#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100660 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101019 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c30919c-c2b9-5e09-8512-98fcbe339564" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing a Profit Function Given a Revenue and Cost Function"}] :question/id #uuid "6c44b63d-72f8-599a-a89c-29fdc2227ab5" :question/math-academy-id "e-357" :question/problem "A company spends $C(x) = x^2 + 80x$ dollars to produce $x$ units of a certain product. The company sells the product for $\\[math] 180$ per unit. What is the maximum monthly profit that the factory can make given that it can produce at most $60$ units of product per month?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ units of the product per month, the company has a monthly revenue (income) of\n\n$$\nR(x)=180\\cdot x=180 x\n$$\n\ndollars.\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(x) &= R(x) - C(x) \\\\\n&= 180x - (x^{2} + 80x) \\\\\n&= 100x - x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the question statement, we know that the permitted domain is\n\n$$\n0\\leq x \\leq 60\n$$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0 < x < 60$. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\nP'(x)=100-2x\n$$\n\nand solving $P'(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP^{′}(x) &= 0 \\\\\n100 - 2x &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 50\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=50$ lies in the interval $0 < x < 60$. All that's left now is to confirm that $x=50$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x)$. To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\nP''(x) = -2 < 0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=50$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x)$. And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=50$ is a global maximum of $P(x)$.\n\nTherefore, the maximum monthly profit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(50) &= 100(50) - (50)^{2} = \\[math]2500\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100661 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101017 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1aca5854-177d-5ebe-9a63-caaa7d916c4d" :knowledge-point/title "Optimizing a Profit, Revenue, or Cost Function"}] :question/id #uuid "e47dca19-6772-532a-9955-ebe6f3db0d28" :question/math-academy-id "e-359" :question/problem "The director of a newspaper estimates that the company's profit, in hundreds of dollars, is given by\n$P(x) = -x^2 + 44x + 5$,\nwhere $x$ is the number of newspapers, in thousands, produced daily. Given that the company has the capacity to produce $30\\,000$ newspapers every day, how many newspapers per day should the company produce to maximize profit?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we note that the permitted domain is\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\leq 30\n$$\n\nsince $x = 30$ corresponds to\n\n$$\n30 \\cdot 1\\,000 = 30\\,000\n$$\n\ncopies of the newspaper. Therefore, $x=0$ and $x = 30$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $P(x)$.\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0 < x < 30$. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\nP'(x) = -2x + 44\n$$\n\nand solving $P'(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP^{′}(x) &= 0 \\\\\n- 2x + 44 &= 0 \\\\\n2x &= 44 \\\\\nx &= 22\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=22$ lies in the interval\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\leq 30\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $x=22$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x)$. To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\nP''(x) = -2 < 0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=22$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x)$. And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=22$ is a global maximum of $P(x)$.\n\nTherefore, the company should produce\n\n$$\n22 \\cdot 1\\,000 = 22\\,000\n$$\n\ncopies of the newspaper per day to maximize its profit."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100784 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101020 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d2b1036-0ea4-5fd1-bfed-885dcbae618b" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing a Profit Function by Constructing a Revenue and Cost Function"}] :question/id #uuid "a63d3bdb-ca20-51f5-b876-7ec8950a46dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-7574" :question/problem "A hotel has $21$ rooms. The manager has observed that when the room rate is $\\[math]12$ per night, all rooms are occupied and, for every $\\[/math]1$ increase in the rate, a single room is vacated. If the maintenance of each rented room is $\\[math]3$ per night, what rate per room should the manager set to obtain the maximum profit, and what is the maximum profit?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that if there are $x$ *vacant* rooms, the hotel has $(21-x)$ occupied rooms, and the rate per room is $(12+x)$ dollars. So, the total revenue (in dollars) is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(x) &= (21 - x)_(⏟)_(occupied rooms) \\cdot (12 + x)_(⏟)_(rate per room) \\\\\n&= 252 + 21x - 12x - x^{2} \\\\\n&= 252 + 9x - x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, for having $(21-x)$ rooms rented out, the hotel has a cost (in dollars) of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC(x) &= 3(21 - x) \\\\\n&= 63 - 3x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(x) &= R(x) - C(x) \\\\\nP(x) &= (252 + 9x - x^{2}) - (63 - 3x) \\\\\n&= 189 + 12x - x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince there are $21$ rooms, the permitted domain is\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x\\leq 21\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$ and $x=21$ are candidates for the global maxima of $P(x)$.\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0 \\lt x\\lt 21$. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\nP'(x) = 12 - 2x \\,\n$$\n\nand solving $P'(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP^{′}(x) &= 0 \\\\\n12 - 2x &= 0 \\\\\n2x &= 12 \\\\\nx &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=6$ lies in the interval $0 < x < 21$. All that's left now is to confirm that $x=6$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x)$. To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\nP''(x) = -2 < 0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=6$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x)$. And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=6$ is a global maximum of $P(x)$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the hotel will have a maximum profit when there are $x=6$ vacant rooms.\n\nWe know that the rate per room is $(12+x)$ dollars. If there are $6$ vacant rooms, then the rate per room is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12 + 6 &= \\[math]18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the manager should charge a room rate of $\\[/math]18$ per night."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100788 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101018 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2d19236-d6ea-57da-b2a4-b926fdddb30e" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing a Revenue Function Given a Price Function"}] :question/id #uuid "7996afa5-da25-51e5-915c-5f0acdccb8bd" :question/math-academy-id "e-7697" :question/problem "A textile center sells $x$ hundreds of meters of fabric at a price $p(x)$, in dollars, given by\n$p(x) = x^2 - 15x + 48 + \\dfrac{78}{x}$.\n\nIf the textile center sells between $100$ and $900$ meters of fabric daily, how many meters of fabric must the textile center sell to maximize its revenue?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ hundreds of meters in total, the total revenue $R(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(x) &= x \\cdot p(x) \\\\\n&= x(x^{2} - 15x + 48 + \\frac{78}{x}) \\\\\n&= x^{3} - 15x^{2} + 48x + 78\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that the permitted domain is\n\n$$\n1 \\leq x \\leq 9\n$$\n\nsince $x = 1$ corresponds to\n\n$$\n1 \\cdot 100 = 100\n$$\n\nand $x = 9$ corresponds to\n\n$$\n9 \\cdot 100 = 900\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=1$ and $x=9$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $R(x)$.\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $R(x)$ for $1 < x < 9$. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\nR'(x) = 3x^2 - 30x + 48\n$$\n\nand solving $R'(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR^{′}(x) &= 0 \\\\\n3x^{2} - 30x + 48 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 10x + 16 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 2)(x - 8) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=2$ and $x=8$.\n\nOur candidates are $x=1,2, 8$ and $9$. Computing $R(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(1) &= (1)^{3} - 15(1)^{2} + 48(1) + 78 = 112 \\\\\nR(2) &= (2)^{3} - 15(2)^{2} + 48(2) + 78 = 122✓ \\\\\nR(8) &= (8)^{3} - 15(8)^{2} + 48(8) + 78 = 14 \\\\\nR(9) &= (9)^{3} - 15(9)^{2} + 48(9) + 78 = 24\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to maximize its revenue, the textile center should sell\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot 100 = 200\n$$\n\nmeters of fabric."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101113 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101020 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d2b1036-0ea4-5fd1-bfed-885dcbae618b" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing a Profit Function by Constructing a Revenue and Cost Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7d727ea-08a3-55dd-a017-4d107ec1aa2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098014} {:answer/id #uuid "e882f15d-cbb3-58de-8db6-593afd851087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098015} {:answer/id #uuid "4a9cdd31-c47f-5335-b161-947bd2eae8c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098016} {:answer/id #uuid "51adfce4-2819-5700-8925-9a7b4a6d258d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098017} {:answer/id #uuid "5755991d-f45e-58ee-b0da-3896a698d7fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098018}] :answer-field/id #uuid "9c185337-13a1-5e13-a931-af896063703c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098013}] :question/id #uuid "284fac66-d855-554d-969c-bb28dda1de3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-119091" :question/problem "A summer camp has capacity for $200$ kids. The price for each kid attending the camp is $$300$. The cost of setting up the camp is made up of a fixed cost of $$2000$ plus a cost of $$100$ per kid. How many kids should attend the summer camp to maximize its profit?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101504 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101019 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c30919c-c2b9-5e09-8512-98fcbe339564" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing a Profit Function Given a Revenue and Cost Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94d4c6ac-6a98-5ca6-aacc-c8c91c7c2181" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$200000$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100186} {:answer/id #uuid "b95f6bfc-f019-590c-a20a-055d06c04ab2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$176000$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100187} {:answer/id #uuid "2031fd6c-5f54-5518-bdfb-161c34ad9c95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$107000$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100188} {:answer/id #uuid "38745beb-e99b-5af8-8495-9f307871a20b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$310000$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100189} {:answer/id #uuid "69ab0c33-8f37-5a7e-960b-d2e068a98b8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$243000$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100190}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8efadd76-a965-516a-bd05-82f7d6c9e07e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100185}] :question/id #uuid "f5c03e3f-902a-5f23-a083-877f778bea41" :question/math-academy-id "q-65867" :question/problem "A factory spends $C(x) = x^{4} + 13x$ thousand dollars to manufacture $x$ units of a certain product. The factory sells the product for $$121000$ per unit. What is the maximum monthly profit that the factory can make given that it can produce at most $4$ items per month?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101564 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101019 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c30919c-c2b9-5e09-8512-98fcbe339564" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing a Profit Function Given a Revenue and Cost Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc079cff-d7da-5a87-b324-3586bd45da20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$850$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100546} {:answer/id #uuid "c0a0efd8-50ec-59dd-9e81-0c4bfa6eebc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$640$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100547} {:answer/id #uuid "d055eaa7-a9f5-5d9e-932a-3b0feca2a7ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$483.5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100548} {:answer/id #uuid "d56e880d-b013-5367-8a35-f97835b2241d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$1000$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100549} {:answer/id #uuid "f8616558-a163-56ed-b4e0-530b461b2845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$219.7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100550}] :answer-field/id #uuid "66e4fa29-0429-5e20-b824-7bf28e1113c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100545}] :question/id #uuid "e5396c61-6040-5997-8775-94633d636485" :question/math-academy-id "q-87573" :question/problem "A computer science center organizes a machine learning course. The course is activated if there are at least $3$ participants, and it can be attended by at most $13$ participants. The price for each participant is $$200$. The cost of providing the course is $C(x) = 200x - 4x^{3} + 0.3x^{4}$, where $x$ is the number of participants. What is the maximum profit that the computer science center can make?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
